## What the project remembers, and where

There is no session-wide activity log here, deliberately. Capturing every prompt
and every tool call runs to tens of megabytes a project, will not go in git, and
answers none of the questions people actually ask. "What did my coauthors do
last week", "why did we change that", and "did we ever explore X" are questions
about *decisions*, and a tool-call log records actions. So the decision gets
written down, with its reason, at the moment it is made.

Three committed records carry the project, and between them they answer all
three questions:

1. **`project/global/state.md` and its changelog.** Every substantive change
   gets a line: date, author slug, what changed, and why it is now settled.
   Newest first, append only. Killed ideas go in with the reason they were
   killed. This is how the others see what moved without reading the whole file,
   and it is the first place to look for any of the three questions above.
2. **`project/<author>/logs/brief-*.md`.** The briefs sent to each debate seat —
   the record of which directions were proposed and attacked. Committed.
3. **`project/<author>/logs/runs.jsonl`.** One line per script execution, with
   inputs, outputs, and the commit. This is what ties a number to the run that
   produced it.

Git history sits underneath all three, and `git log --since` answers "last week"
directly.

Two things stay local and are not memory: `logs/debate-<run>.jsonl`, the full
model responses from each debate call, and anything a tool wrote to a scratch
path. Read a slice of the debate log when a specific question needs it —

```bash
python3 -m tools.logging_ --seat adversary --limit 5
```

— but if a voice said something that changed the project's direction, that
belongs in `state.md` with the reason, not left in a log on one machine.

**When you learn something the project should keep, write it down now.** Not at
the end of the session, not in your own words in a scratch file — into
`state.md` with a changelog line, or into the brief, or into the run record.
Nothing reconstructs it later.

A run is stamped with `python3 -m tools.runid --project . --new` at the top of a
round. Files are named by run, never by round: a round counter is per-machine,
so in a shared repo two people each have a round 3, and `<author>-<date>-<time>`
cannot collide.

