## /refresh — checkpoint before the context runs out

When the session gets long, run `/refresh`. It promotes what has settled into
`state.md` with a changelog line, rewrites your `session.md` so a cold session
could resume from it alone, and releases the lock if the round is done. Then it
stops — it cannot clear its own context.

Suggest it proactively when a round has run long.

---

## /status — the project, on one page

`/status` writes an HTML report to the repo root: executive summary, what is
settled, open issues, and a chronology. It reads only the committed records —
`state.md` and its changelog, each `session.md`, the briefs, the run records,
and git — and assembles the timeline with:

```bash
python3 -m tools.chronology --human
python3 -m tools.chronology --since "last week" --kinds changelog,commit --human
python3 -m tools.chronology --author <slug> --human
```

That tool is also the fastest answer to a question asked in passing. "What did
Kevin do last week" is `--author kevin-crotty --since "last week"`. "Why did we
change that" is `--kinds changelog`, because a changelog entry carries its
reason. "Did we ever explore X" is the killed-ideas section of `state.md` plus
`--kinds brief`.

None of that reads a session log, and none of it needs to — which is why the
changelog has to be written when the decision is made, not reconstructed after.

### When the changelog falls behind

You write the changelog, during `/refresh`. `/refresh` only runs when somebody
invokes it, so a session that ends any other way — the context filled up, the
terminal closed, the work was done and everyone moved on — leaves the commits
in git and the reason nowhere. Nothing notices, because the commits look fine.

```bash
python3 -m tools.chronology --gap --human
```

That names the commits with no entry after them. `lock release` runs it for you
and warns; `/refresh` and `/status` both start with it. When it shows a gap,
write the entries while the reason is still recoverable — from this
conversation, from the diff, from `session.md`. Say which ones you cannot
reconstruct instead of inventing a reason: a wrong entry in the changelog is
worse than a missing one, because it will be believed.

